---
/**
 * What this site is, who maintains it, and what it is not.
 *
 * Separate from `/verification/`, which is the mechanism in detail. This page
 * is the one a reader (or a reviewer) opens to find out whether the site is
 * worth trusting; that question is answered by who wrote it and why, not by a
 * description of the build.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { buildInfo } from "../build-info";
import { guideHref, pageHref } from "../nav";
import { CHANNELS, channelUrl } from "../channels";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const { zigVersion, snippetCount } = await buildInfo();
---

<Base
  title="About"
  seoTitle="About Zig Guide Live"
  description="Who writes this Zig guide, why it targets master and not a tagged release, and what the guide is not, by design."
  crumbs={[{ label: "About" }]}
  schema={{ "@type": "AboutPage", name: "About Zig Guide Live" }}
>
  <article>
    <h1>About this guide</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Zig Guide Live is a tutorial and cookbook for the Zig programming
      language. Before this page was published, a machine compiled and ran
      every code example on the site against Zig {zigVersion}. When you press
      Run, your browser downloads the same artifact that ran in that check.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Zig has no stable release, and the language still changes. Much of the
      text about Zig becomes out of date. These changes occurred between the
      last tagged releases and today:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>pub fn main()</code> got a parameter.</li>
      <li>Writers became buffered, and you must flush them.</li>
      <li>
        <code>std.fs.File</code> became <code>std.Io.File</code>, and it now
        takes an <code>Io</code>.
      </li>
      <li>The <code>**</code> array-repeat operator was removed completely.</li>
      <li><code>ArrayList</code> does not hold an allocator now.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each of these changes breaks the first program in most Zig tutorials
      that exist.
    </p>
    <p>
      Usually this failure is silent. An author writes a tutorial against the
      compiler that the author had. The tutorial is correct for a few months.
      Then it is wrong, with no visible sign that something changed. A reader
      who follows the tutorial gets an error message that is not in the text.
      The reader cannot tell if the reader or the page made the mistake.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site makes the failure visible. The prose keeps no separate copy of
      the examples. The pages embed the same files that the build compiles, so
      a snippet that stopped working cannot be published. When Zig master
      changes something, the build fails. The fix is to teach the new form,
      and to say what the old form was. For a reader who comes from an older
      tutorial, that difference is usually the most useful part of the page.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      The repository keeps {snippetCount} snippets as ordinary
      <code>.zig</code> files. A build step compiles all of them, and runs the
      snippets that can run. Then the build step compares their output with a
      recorded expected result. The site builds only if all of these checks
      pass, and only then is the site published. The same check runs again
      every night against a new compiler. Thus the guide proves that it is
      correct today, not only on the day of the last edit.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the full mechanism, read{" "}
      <a href={`${base}verification/`}>how this guide is verified</a>. That
      page also tells what occurs to the snippets that cannot run in a browser,
      and to the five snippets that must fail.
    </p>

    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <p>
      Rajesh Pillai writes this guide. It is the work of one person, written
      with AI assistance and then verified. The build above verifies every
      code example. A team of engineers verifies the prose before it is
      published. The machine checks that the code is correct. People check
      that the explanation is correct.
    </p>
    <p>
      This guide is not affiliated with the Zig Software Foundation. The
      official language documentation is at{" "}
      <a href="https://ziglang.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >ziglang.org</a>. If the two disagree on a point, the official
      documentation is correct.
    </p>
    <p>
      The source is public at{" "}
      <a
        href="https://github.com/rajeshpillai/zig-guide"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/rajeshpillai/zig-guide</a>, with
      every snippet and the build that checks them. If something here is wrong,
      anyone can check and correct it in public. See{" "}
      <a href={`${base}contact/`}>contact</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same author also makes short riddle and joke videos on YouTube:{" "}
      {CHANNELS.map((channel, i) => (
        <>
          {i > 0 && ", "}
          <a href={channelUrl(channel)} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >{channel.name}</a>
        </>
      ))}. Each chapter ends with one of these videos, and the video plays on
      the page.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Not a language reference.</strong> The guide runs programs, and
        then explains what they did. If you need the exact rule, use the
        official reference and the standard library source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not a guide to a tagged release.</strong> The code here
        targets master. On 0.14 or 0.15, much of the code does not build. For
        that problem, read{" "}
        <a href={pageHref("getting-started/coming-from-older-zig")}
          >Migrate from an Older Zig</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not stable in the way a book is.</strong> The chapters change
        when the compiler changes. The version in the footer is always the
        compiler that verified the page that you read. The page{" "}
        <a href={`${base}whats-new/`}>what's new</a> lists the new chapters.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not published without review.</strong> The drafts use AI
        assistance, but no draft goes on the site for that reason alone. The
        build proves the code. Engineers read the prose, and they can tell if
        the explanation is correct.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reading it</h2>
    <p>
      There are seven tracks, and one reading order through all of them. The
      arrows at the bottom of every chapter follow that order, so you can read
      the guide from start to end. If you know what you want,{" "}
      <a href={`${base}paths/`}>the reading paths</a> give a route for seven
      common starting points. <a href={guideHref}>The guide index</a> lists
      every section.
    </p>

    <p><a href={guideHref}>Read the guide →</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
